      "text": "To determine the measure of angle \\( P \\), we need to analyze the given angles and the relationships between them. Let's denote the measure of angle \\( P \\) as \\( x \\).\n\nFirst, observe that the sum of the interior angles of a pentagon (which is the shape of the pentagon \\( ABCDE \\)) is given by the formula:\n\\[ \\text{Sum of interior angles} = (n-2) \\times 180^\\circ, \\]\nwhere \\( n \\) is the number of sides of the polygon. For a pentagon, \\( n = 5 \\), so:\n\\[ \\text{Sum of interior angles} = (5-2) \\times 180^\\circ = 3 \\times 180^\\circ = 540^\\circ. \\]\n\nThe angles given in the problem are \\( 128^\\circ, 92^\\circ, 113^\\circ, \\) and \\( 111^\\circ \\). Let's add these angles:\n\\[ 128^\\circ + 92^\\circ + 113^\\circ + 111^\\circ = 444^\\circ. \\]\n\nSince the sum of the interior angles of the pentagon is \\( 540^\\circ \\), the remaining angle quantity must equal the difference between 540 and 444:\n\\[ 540^\\circ - 444^\\circ = 96^\\circ. \\]\n\nThis remaining \\( 96^\\circ \\) is the angle \\( P \\). Therefore, the measure of angle \\( P \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{96^\\circ}. \\]",
