      "text": "To find the measure of angle \\( P \\) in the given regular pentagon, we need to understand the properties of a regular pentagon's angles. In a regular pentagon, each internal angle is equal. The sum of the internal angles of a pentagon is given by the formula \\( (n-2) \\times 180^\\circ \\), where \\( n \\) is the number of sides. For a pentagon (\\( n = 5 \\)), this would be \\( (5-2) \\times 180^\\circ = 3 \\times 180^\\circ = 540^\\circ \\). \n\nSince a regular pentagon has equal internal angles, each internal angle measures \\( \\frac{540^\\circ}{5} \\). \n\nWe can use this formula to find the measure of angle \\( P \\):\n\\[ \\text{Angle at vertex } P = \\frac{540^\\circ}{5} = 108^\\circ \\]\n\nNow, let's put this reasoning to the test by writing and running the corresponding Python code.\n```python\n# Number of sides in a regular pentagon\nn = 5\n\n# Sum of internal angles of a pentagon\nsum_of_angles = (n - 2) * 180\n\n# Each internal angle in a regular pentagon\ninternal_angle = sum_of_angles / n\nprint(internal_angle)\n```\n```output\n108.0\n```\nThe measure of angle \\( P \\) in the given pentagon is \\(\\boxed{108}\\) degrees.",
